Template: homelab-roundcube/db_host
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: PostgreSQL host to bootstrap the split runtime/migrate roles on:
 Used once, at install time, to create the narrow homelab_roundcube_runtime/
 homelab_roundcube_migrate role pair and its own "roundcube" schema (see
 CLAUDE.md's split-role design). Needs direct Postgres access (peer auth
 as the postgres OS user, locally or over SSH) — this is NOT the host
 Roundcube connects to at runtime, see pgbouncer_host below.

Template: homelab-roundcube/pgbouncer_host
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: PgBouncer host for Roundcube's own runtime database connection:
 Roundcube's db_dsnw connects here at runtime (port 6432), never straight
 to Postgres — see CLAUDE.md's split runtime/migrate role design. The
 bootstrapped runtime role is also registered into this host's pgbouncer
 userlist.txt.

Template: homelab-roundcube/imap_host
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: IMAP host (homelab-dovecot):
 Written into config.inc.php's imap_host as ssl://<host>:<port> --
 Roundcube and Dovecot no longer need to be co-located (see
 homelab-mailbridge's identical split). Leave as localhost only when
 genuinely co-installed.

Template: homelab-roundcube/imap_port
Type: string
Default: 993
Description: IMAP port (TLS):

Template: homelab-roundcube/smtp_host
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: SMTP submission host (homelab-postfix):
 Written into config.inc.php's smtp_host as tls://<host>:<port>. Leave
 as localhost only when genuinely co-installed.

Template: homelab-roundcube/smtp_port
Type: string
Default: 587
Description: SMTP submission port:

Template: homelab-roundcube/internal_port
Type: string
Default: 8080
Description: Internal loopback port for Roundcube's own nginx+PHP-FPM vhost:
 homelab-webproxy's own sites.yml reverse-proxies to this port — must
 match whatever "upstream: 127.0.0.1:<port>" is configured there for
 Roundcube's domain.

Template: homelab-roundcube/product_name
Type: string
Default: Homelab Webmail
Description: Product name shown on the login screen and window title:

Template: homelab-roundcube/sso_base_url
Type: string
Default: http://127.0.0.1:2502
Description: Base URL where homelab-sso is reachable:
 Written into config.inc.php's oauth_auth_uri/oauth_token_uri/
 oauth_identity_uri/oauth_logout_uri. homelab-sso is the only place a
 user ever types a password -- this app's native OAuth support (plus
 two small core patches, see script/homelab-roundcube-patch-oauth-sso)
 redirects here for login and logout.
 .
 IMPORTANT once this app and homelab-sso are on different hosts:
 oauth_auth_uri and oauth_logout_uri are BROWSER redirects, not
 server-to-server calls -- this value must be an address the end
 user's own browser can actually reach (e.g. homelab-sso's own public
 homelab-webproxy vhost, https://login.example.com), never an internal-
 only data-plane IP/port. A raw internal address here will produce a
 login page that redirects somewhere the browser can't connect to at
 all -- found live, not just in review, the first time this was set to
 an internal IP instead of the public vhost. (oauth_token_uri/
 oauth_identity_uri are genuinely server-side and would work fine
 either way; this one value drives all four, so it has to satisfy the
 stricter browser-facing requirement.)

Template: homelab-roundcube/sso_internal_base_url
Type: string
Default:
Description: Internal (server-to-server) base URL for homelab-sso (blank = same as the public URL above):
 Written into config.inc.php's oauth_token_uri/oauth_identity_uri only --
 the two OAuth calls this app's own PHP process makes directly, never
 through the end user's browser. Leave blank on a single-host install
 (falls back to homelab-roundcube/sso_base_url, unchanged behavior). Set
 this explicitly, to an internal address like http://10.50.2.48:2502,
 once this app and homelab-sso are on separate hosts -- confirmed live
 (2026-09-25) that reusing the PUBLIC URL for these two calls breaks
 once that's true: each instance's own outbound HTTP call to the public
 vhost has to hairpin back through the edge proxy to reach a sibling
 host, which doesn't work in this environment (fails as "Connection
 refused", non-deterministically across a load-balanced pool depending
 on which instance handles a given login). See
 config.inc.php.template's own comment on this split for the full
 story.

Template: homelab-roundcube/sso_client_secret
Type: password
Description: Shared secret for this app's "roundcube" OAuth client:
 Must match exactly what homelab-sso's own install printed for the
 "roundcube" client (dpkg-reconfigure homelab-sso to see it again, or
 rotate it). Unlike homelab-sso's own client secrets, there's no
 sensible default to auto-generate here -- this app is the consumer of
 a secret homelab-sso mints, not the minter.

Template: homelab-roundcube/api_base_url
Type: string
Default: http://127.0.0.1:3000
Description: Base URL where homelab-api is reachable:
 Used only by the recipient_blocking plugin (block/unblock one of your
 own addresses from the message toolbar / Settings -> Blocked
 Addresses), which calls homelab-api's own /api/v1/domains/recipient-
 access/mine self-service endpoints using the same JWT this app's
 native OAuth login already holds in $_SESSION['oauth_token'] for
 IMAP/SMTP -- no separate credential. Same-host loopback by default,
 like sso_base_url above.

Template: homelab-roundcube/cipher_key
Type: password
Default:
Description: Shared session-encryption key (blank = auto-generate a new random one):
 Roundcube uses this to encrypt/decrypt $_SESSION['password'] (the IMAP/
 SMTP credential this app's own OAuth login stores per-session) inside
 whatever session_storage backend is configured -- 'db' here, so every
 pool member reads and writes the SAME session rows in Postgres (see
 config.inc.php.template's own session_storage comment; this is
 deliberate, NOT session affinity, so any instance can serve any
 request in a session). That sharing only works if every instance
 decrypts with the SAME key it was encrypted with.
 .
 Leave blank for a single, standalone instance -- each install then
 auto-generates its own fresh random key exactly like every install
 before this template existed, and nothing above matters.
 .
 IMPORTANT for an HA pool (see homelab-roundcube/instance_id above):
 leaving this blank on more than one instance means EACH ONE generates
 its own independently-random key, so a session written by one instance
 can never be decrypted by another -- found live, 2026-09-26: this
 produced "Connection to storage server failed" / "Server Error: Empty
 password" on roughly 2 of every 3 requests across a real 3-instance
 pool, the exact same class of bug instance_id's own description
 documents for the Postgres role (independent random values instead of
 one shared value). Generate ONE value yourself (e.g. `openssl rand
 -base64 32 | head -c 32`) and give every pool member's install the
 SAME value here. Changing this on an already-bootstrapped instance
 does nothing (config.inc.php is only written once -- see the
 "$CONFIG_FILE already bootstrapped" message); edit
 $config['des_key'] in /etc/roundcube/config.inc.php by hand on every
 instance instead, then restart each instance's php-fpm.

Template: homelab-roundcube/instance_id
Type: string
Default:
Description: Unique identifier for this HA instance's own Postgres role:
 Only needed when running more than one homelab-roundcube instance
 behind the same webproxy upstream pool (see homelab-webproxy's own
 sites.yml) -- each instance needs its OWN Postgres role, or every new
 instance's install silently rotates the shared role's password out
 from under every other already-running instance (confirmed live: this
 broke webmail login fleet-wide -- "Internal Error" on roughly 2 of
 every 3 requests -- during a real 3-instance rebuild). Leave blank for
 a single, standalone instance (keeps the plain homelab_roundcube_
 runtime role name, matching every install before this template
 existed). For an HA pool, give each instance its own short, stable
 value (e.g. its own hostname: "ct14", "ct15", "ct16") -- the role
 becomes homelab_roundcube_<value>_runtime. All instances still share
 the SAME "roundcube" schema/data (contacts, preferences, message
 cache) -- only the credential is per-instance, never the data; a user
 sees the same mailbox state no matter which instance answers their
 request. Changing this on an already-bootstrapped instance does NOT
 migrate the old role; remove /etc/roundcube/config.inc.php.bootstrapped
 and run 'dpkg-reconfigure homelab-roundcube' afterward to bootstrap
 the new one.
